  "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{\u2217}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{\u2217},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\u21d2\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{\u2032}={u}^{\u2032}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{\u2032}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{\u2032} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{\u2032}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
